==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|grc|χᾰ́ρῐσμᾰ||grace, favour, gift}}, from {{m|grc|χᾰρῐ́ζομαι||to show favor}}, from {{m|grc|χᾰ́ρῐς||grace}}, from the same root {{m|grc||χαρ-}} as {{m|grc|χαίρω||to be happy}}. {{doublet|en|charism}}. {{etydate|1875}}.

Outside of theology, as early as 1930, ''charisma'' was found in a translation of a book written by German sociologist {{w|Max Weber}}, which ''OED'' and ''Etymonline''<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> regards as a {{sl|en|de|Charisma|nocap=1}}, denoting the special ability of certain leaders to inspire devotion ({{senseno|en|gift of leadership}}). By the 1940s and more clearly in the 1950s, the term was used more loosely to refer to personal charm in general ({{senseno|en|charm}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kəˈɹɪzmə/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Wodencafe-charisma.wav|a=Texas}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-charisma.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s|charismata}}

# {{senseid|en|gift of leadership}} A [[gift]] or [[power]] of [[leadership]] or [[authority]]. {{defdate|from 1930}}
#: {{syn|en|magnetism}}
#: {{uxi|en|The leader has his '''charisma'''.}}
## {{senseid|en|charm}} The [[capacity]] to inspire [[devotion]] or [[enthusiasm]]; personal [[charm]]. {{defdate|from 1959}}
##: {{uxi|en|the '''charisma''' of a popular actor}}
# {{lb|en|Christianity|theology}} A [[gift]] or [[power]] bestowed upon a person by the [[Holy Spirit]]; a [[grace]]. {{defdate|from 1875}}
#: {{syn|en|charism}}

